# Licensing

WilderThings is free and open source. It is licensed in two parts, because a
repository of survival guides is mostly writing with a little bit of tooling
around it, and those two things want different licenses.

## The guides (content) — CC BY-SA 4.0

Everything in `docs/`, plus `templates/`, is licensed under the
**Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License**.

Full text: [LICENSE-CONTENT.txt](LICENSE-CONTENT.txt)
Summary: <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/>

You are free to:

- **Share** — copy and redistribute the material in any medium or format
- **Adapt** — remix, transform, and build upon the material

for any purpose, **including commercially**. Print it, translate it, put it on
a USB stick, hand it out, fold it into a course, sell the printed copies.

Under these terms:

- **Attribution** — credit "WilderThings Contributors", link to the license,
  and indicate if you made changes.
- **ShareAlike** — if you remix or build upon it, distribute your contributions
  under this same license.
- **No additional restrictions** — you may not apply legal terms or
  technological measures that legally restrict others from doing anything the
  license permits.

ShareAlike is deliberate: it keeps this collection free for everyone downstream.
Nobody can take these guides, improve them, and lock the result away.

## The tooling (code) — MIT

The build and configuration code is licensed under the **MIT License**.

Full text: [LICENSE-CODE.txt](LICENSE-CODE.txt)

This covers:

- `mkdocs.yml`, `mkdocs.offline.yml`
- `scripts/` (`build-offline.sh`, `generate-icons.py`)
- `build_single_file.py`
- `overrides/`
- `docs/assets/css/`
- `.github/` workflows and configuration

MIT keeps the tooling trivially reusable — copy the offline build setup into
your own MkDocs project without inheriting ShareAlike obligations.

## Third-party components

- **MkDocs Material** — MIT License, © Martin Donath. See
  <https://github.com/squidfunk/mkdocs-material>.
- **`docs/assets/javascripts/iframe-worker.shim.js`** — MIT License, from the
  `iframe-worker` package by Martin Donath. Vendored so the offline build makes
  no network requests.

## Sources cited in the guides

The guides cite external references — field manuals, medical guidelines,
textbooks. Those works remain under their own copyright and are **not**
relicensed here. Citing a source is not redistributing it. Where a guide
paraphrases a procedure, the wording is original; where a specific protocol is
named (TCCC, START triage, ATLS), the underlying protocol belongs to its issuing
body.

## No warranty

This material is provided as-is, with no warranty of any kind. See the
disclaimer in [README.md](README.md) and the content accuracy policy in
[SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).

**This is reference material, not a substitute for training, professional
medical care, or emergency services.**
